**Approach.** Push onto an **in** stack. To pop or peek, take from an **out** stack; when it is empty, pour the whole in-stack into it (which reverses the order, so the oldest element ends on top).

```python
class QueueWithTwoStacks:
    def __init__(self):
        self._in, self._out = [], []

    def push(self, x):
        self._in.append(x)

    def _fill(self):
        if not self._out:
            while self._in:
                self._out.append(self._in.pop())

    def pop(self):
        self._fill()
        return self._out.pop()

    def peek(self):
        self._fill()
        return self._out[-1]

    def empty(self):
        return not self._in and not self._out
```

**Complexity.** Each element is moved at most once from one stack to the other, so every operation is **amortised O(1)**, even though a single pop can take O(n). In Python, `collections.deque` is the real answer for a queue; say so after you answer the question asked.

**Approach.** Put one pointer at each end, skip characters that do not count, and compare the rest, moving inwards. No cleaned-up copy of the string is needed, so extra space is O(1).

